  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 120 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA nearby upper-level system sends a weak front into west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico Wednesday. This front will do little more than \nshift winds northerly again, and will not have much cooler air \nbehind it. Highs Wednesday through Friday top out in the upper 60s \nand low-to-mid 70s, which is near to just above normal for mid-\nNovember. Rain chances will be nil through Friday night thanks to \nthe aforementioned dry front and ridging to our west. However, by \nFriday night, another upper-level system approaches west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico from the west. Moisture will increase across \nthe area ahead of the system, yielding additional low rain chances \n(10-30%) Saturday morning into Sunday. Right now, it looks like the \nrelatively better rain chances would be for areas along and south of \nI-10. Lower heights associated with the incoming trough allow \ntemperatures to drop a couple of degrees Saturday and Sunday, with \nmost locations topping out in the mid-to-upper 60s and low 70s.\n\nSprang",
